# world harness — every environment variable this harness reads, in one file.
#
#   install -m 600 harness.env.example harness.env    # owner-only; then fill in values
#
# `load_harness_env()` reads env/harness.env on `import world` (every Harness
# construction), so nothing needs exporting in the launching shell. harness.env is
# gitignored; never commit real tokens, never paste them into a chat, and never `cat`
# this file where its output is logged. The loader warns if the file is readable by
# anyone but you (`chmod 600 env/harness.env` fixes it). Agent subprocesses (the
# claude / codex CLI, their Bash, plugin MCP servers) do NOT inherit these values:
# world/env.py child_env() strips them; only the harness's own in-process tools read them.
#
# NAMING: <PROJECT>_<THING> for anything this harness owns; a bare <THING> for a
# value that is genuinely shared across a set of harnesses (the approver is one human
# — one identity, one variable). Replace <PROJECT> below with your harness's prefix.
#
# There is no kit-wide prefix on these names (an earlier layout prefixed every one).
# Your harness's own `<PROJECT>_` is what keeps its values apart from another
# harness's. The bare shared names are the ones that can collide with something
# already exported in your shell — and the shell WINS, because the loader only fills in
# what isn't already set. It says so out loud when that happens ("shadowed by the
# environment"); if you see that line and didn't mean it, `unset` the shell variable
# rather than renaming the one in here.
#
# ⚠️ Never set ANTHROPIC_API_KEY. Its absence is what keeps the agent SDK on the
# `claude` CLI subscription login instead of metered API billing. The loader itself does
# not filter it, but a claude-provider harness warns at startup when it is set, and
# ClaudeRunner refuses to run any agent while it is (world/runners/claude.py); it is
# also never passed to an agent subprocess.


# ── shared ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The single human approver + escalation target. Widening this ("single approver, no
# exceptions") means widening a core invariant — do it deliberately, not by copy.
APPROVER_SLACK_ID=U0XXXXXXX

# Signs a relayed approval decision, so a BOT co-approver can only carry a decision a
# human actually made (world/slack/approval_text.py HMACs thread|decision|msg_ts with
# this). Only needed if you set a co_approver. Unset means no bot may relay.
#   python3 -c 'import secrets; print(secrets.token_hex(32))'
RELAY_SECRET=


# ── <PROJECT> (your harness) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# bin/slack-provision.py fills in every token below (main + personas) through the
# Slack CLI; the values here are only placeholders until then.
# Main persona: the ONE Socket Mode app (bot xoxb + app-level xapp).
<PROJECT>_SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=xoxb-...
<PROJECT>_SLACK_APP_TOKEN=xapp-...    # Socket Mode app-level token (CLI-issued, or UI-issued once)
<PROJECT>_SLACK_CHANNEL=C0XXXXXXX     # the ONE dedicated bot channel

# Optional: a relay/proxy BOT whose decision is honored alongside the human approver.
# It answers by POSTING a decision message, not clicking (Slack emits block_actions
# only from real UI). Leave unset unless you run a relaying sibling harness.
# <PROJECT>_CO_APPROVER_SLACK_ID=U0XXXXXXX

# Optional: comma-separated channel IDs where an @-mention still wakes this harness up
# (e.g. a sibling harness's channel). Replies always land in the dedicated channel.
# <PROJECT>_RELAY_CHANNELS=C0XXXXXXX,C0YYYYYYY

# Optional, rare: a persona with a Slack app of its own (slack-apps.json lists it).
# Every other persona posts through the main app under its own name; it needs no token.
# <PROJECT>_PERSONA_<NAME>_TOKEN=xoxb-...

# [only channel=discord]
# EXPERIMENTAL — Discord instead of Slack (docs/discord.md). Only read when harness.py
# passes `discord=DiscordConfig(...)`; these are DiscordConfig's default names.
# Check them with: uv run python bin/discord-setup.py
# DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN=...                # Developer Portal → Bot → Reset Token
# DISCORD_CHANNEL_ID=000000000000000000 # the ONE text channel (right-click → Copy Channel ID)
# APPROVER_DISCORD_ID=000000000000000000 # the ONE user whose button click approves
# [/only]
